e":"conceptual","question":"How can $v_2$ itself be written as a linear combination of $(v_1,v_2,v_3)$?","options":["$v_1+v_3$","$0v_1+0v_2+0v_3$","$1v_1+1v_2+1v_3$","$0v_1+1v_2+0v_3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f333d160b209ffce6fa57058","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_manual_batch1_linear_combination_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Do coefficients in a linear combination have to be unique?","options":["Yes, any list of vectors gives unique coefficients once the vector being written is fixed.","Not in general; uniqueness requires extra conditions such as linear independence.","They are unique only when all vectors are zero.","No, unless all coefficients are positive."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f5ea0ec654ad0255e903bb5a","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_manual_batch1_linear_combination_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Compute $2(1,2)-1(3,0)$.","options":["$(5,4)$","$(-1,4)$","$(-1,2)$","$(1,2)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"58c32a5bb40277650579069e","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_manual_batch1_linear_combination_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which vector is not a linear combination of the single vector $(1,0)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$?","options":["$(0,1)$","$(0,0)$","$(2,0)$","$(-3,0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"192a2affa67034fecd4de276","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_manual_batch1_linear_combination_020_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Find scalars $a,b$ with $a(1,0)+b(1,1)=(2,3)$.","options":["$a=-1$, $b=3$","$a=3$, $b=-1$","$a=2$, $b=3$","$a=1$, $b=2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f33c142c491c7d7b2780fcbf","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_linear_combination_03957550_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Is the vector $(1,3)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ a linear combination of $\\boldsymbol{v}_1=(1,2)$ and $\\boldsymbol{v}_2=(2,4)$?","options":["Yes, with coefficients $\\alpha_1=3$ and $\\alpha_2=-1$","Yes, as two nonzero vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ reach every vector","No, as every combination of $\\boldsymbol{v}_1$ and $\\boldsymbol{v}_2$ lies on the line $y=2x$","No, as $\\boldsymbol{v}_2$ is a multiple of $\\boldsymbol{v}_1$, so only $\\boldsymbol{0}$ is a combination of them"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c59e56b324e2136ab684881","time_limit":40}]}}